bun-1.4.3-4-powerpc64le.pkg.tar.zst -- held back 2026-10-08, NOT POWER8-legal.

Measured 349,172 ISA 3.0 instructions by tools/isa30-pkgscan.sh. For contrast the
POWER9 build (bun-1.4.3-3, repo/) scans 347,558 -- i.e. the baseline build came
out with essentially the same ISA as the POWER9 one, so nothing POWER8 took
effect for the bulk of the code.

The instructions are real, not a jump-table artifact: stxv 114906, lxv 93936,
stxvx 47608, lxvx 29288, vextubrx 18087, vextuhrx 7159, xxspltib 6370,
lxsd 4835, xxperm 4593, cnttzd 4137, extswsli 3144, maddld 2576, mtvsrdd 2274,
mfvsrld 1876, stxsd 1270. Sample encoding 0xf7ff004d is primary opcode 61,
i.e. stxv (DQ-form, ISA 3.0), and it appears in function prologues as VSX
callee-save -- ordinary compiler codegen, behind no runtime check.

Not the LLInt. The occurrences are spread uniformly over 62 of the 60.3 MiB of
.text; an offlineasm-generated interpreter would be one contiguous block. So
ours/webkit's --ppc64-isa gating is not what failed here.

Cause: ours/bun has no ISA pinning at all. It does not thread CFLAGS/CXXFLAGS
into the cmake it drives via scripts/build.ts, and sets nothing for zig
(-Dcpu=, see the zig-builds-on-this-distro note: -Dcpu=baseline is the pin) or
for cargo. tools/makepkg.conf.d/00-power8.conf WAS installed and its own asserts
passed, so makepkg's CFLAGS/CXXFLAGS/RUSTFLAGS were POWER8 -- bun's own build
system simply does not read them.

Do not publish. The POWER9 build in repo/ is correct for POWER9 and unaffected.
